{{nihongo|'''Masaaki Fukuda'''|福田 正秋|Fukuda Masaaki|born January 14, 1939}} is a Sanese lawyer and politician who served as [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for 48 days from March 28 to May 15, 1991. Leading the first non-Progressive Party government in the post-war era, Fukuda presided over the initial collapse of the economic bubble that inaugurated Okaiken's "[[Lost Decade|Lost Decades]]." The son of [[Takeo Fukuda]], the 34th Prime Minister (1977–1979), Masaaki Fukuda led a fragile multi-party coalition that fractured under the weight of the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal|Miyazaki scandal's]] political aftershocks and the sudden implosion of asset prices in early 1991.

His premiership, the shortest of any post-war prime minister, became emblematic of the governmental paralysis that characterized Okaiken's political landscape throughout the 1990s. The [[OSE 225]] index plummeted from its all-time high of 39,000 in December 1990 to 30,000 within weeks of his taking office, a collapse his unwieldy coalition government proved utterly incapable of addressing.

==Early life and education==
{{See also|Takeo Fukuda}}
[[File:Kagami City Hall.jpg|thumb|left|200px|The Kagami Municipal Building, where Fukuda's father began his political career.]]
Masaaki Fukuda was born on January 14, 1939, in the city of [[Kagami]], [[Kagami Prefecture]], as the eldest son of [[Takeo Fukuda]], then a mid-level bureaucrat in the Ministry of Finance who would later serve as Prime Minister. His mother, {{nihongo|Sachiko Fukuda|福田幸子|Fukuda Sachiko}}, was a homemaker from a merchant family in [[Mito]].

The Fukuda family had deep roots in Kagami's commercial establishment. Masaaki's paternal grandfather, {{nihongo|Genzō Fukuda|福田源蔵}}, operated a successful sake brewing enterprise that had served the region since the late 19th century. This economic security afforded Masaaki a privileged upbringing despite the wartime deprivations that affected Okaiken during his early childhood.

Fukuda attended Kagami Elementary School before enrolling at the prestigious [[Okami University]] Preparatory School in 1954. An unremarkable but diligent student, he matriculated to Okami University's Faculty of Law in 1958, studying constitutional law under the tutelage of Professor [[Noboru Tanaka]], a influential scholar who encouraged several of his students to pursue careers in public service.

While at university, Fukuda became involved in student political organizations, initially joining the liberal Okami Student Alliance before pivoting toward the centrist Democratic Youth Federation following his father's election to the Diet in 1962. He graduated in 1963 with a Bachelor of Laws degree and passed the national bar examination the following year.

==Early political career==
===Legal career and entry into politics===
After completing his legal apprenticeship at the [[Okami Bar Association]], Fukuda established a modest practice specializing in administrative law in [[Okami]], handling cases primarily involving land use disputes and local government regulations. He maintained a careful distance from national politics during his father's ascent through the Progressive Party ranks, seeking to establish his own professional identity independent of the Fukuda name.

His father's brief premiership from 1977 to 1979 complicated Masaaki's political calculations. While Takeo Fukuda's tenure ended in electoral disappointment, the elder Fukuda's reputation for clean government and fiscal conservatism remained untarnished by the personal corruption scandals that would later haunt Okaiken's political establishment. Masaaki leveraged this legacy in 1980, securing the Komeitō nomination for Kagami 1st district following the retirement of the incumbent representative.

===Kagami 1st constituency===
Fukuda won election to the [[Diet of Okaiken|House of Representatives]] in the [[1980 Okaiken general election|1980 general election]] with 42% of the vote, defeating both the Progressive Party incumbent and a Liberal Democratic Party challenger. His campaign emphasized administrative reform, environmental protection of Kagami Prefecture's coastal waters, and continued adherence to his father's "clean government" philosophy.

In the Diet, Fukuda positioned himself as a pragmatic centrist, avoiding the ideologically charged rhetoric that characterized late-Honda Shogunate era parliamentary discourse. He joined the Committee on Budget and the Committee on Land, Infrastructure and Transport, developing expertise in public works financing that would later inform his controversial—but never implemented—stimulus proposals during the 1991 economic crisis.

===Rise to party leadership===
By 1985, Fukuda had emerged as a leading voice within the [[Komeitō]] party's moderate faction. Following the resignation of party leader [[Takeshi Akiyama]] amid internal factional disputes, Fukuda was elected as the new leader at age 46, making him one of the youngest party leaders in modern Okaiken history.

As party leader, Fukuda brokered an alliance with the Okaiken Socialist Party, the Democratic Alliance for Progressive Opposition (DAPO), and several minor parties to form an anti-LDP electoral coalition ahead of the anticipated 1991 elections. This "United Front" strategy initially appeared promising as public dissatisfaction with the ruling Liberal Democratic Party grew following revelations of the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] in late 1990.

==Premiership (1991)==
{{Main|Fukuda cabinet}}
===Appointment===
[[File:OSE 225 Historical Chart 1990-1991.png|thumb|right|300px|The precipitous decline of the [[OSE 225]] index between January and May 1991, which dominated Fukuda's brief tenure.]]

The [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]], which exposed systemic corruption linking LDP leaders, construction magnates, and organized crime, forced the resignation of Prime Minister [[Kentaro Tokuyama]] in October 1990. The subsequent coalition government led by [[Honda Shigehara]] of the Progressive Party of Okaiken collapsed in early 1991 when socialist partners withdrew their support over proposed austerity measures.

On March 28, 1991, Emperor Wakayama, acting on the advice of the Privy Council, appointed Fukuda as the compromise candidate to lead a broad but deeply fractured anti-LDP coalition comprising Komeitō, the Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP), the Democratic Alliance for Progressive Opposition (DAPO), the Progressive Party of Okaiken (PPO), the New Trade Democrats (NTD), and the Social Democratic Party of Okaiken (SDPO).

