The cabinet's ideological incoherence proved immediately problematic. Finance Minister Kamei, representing the Socialist Party's austerity wing, opposed the deficit spending that Infrastructure Minister Takahata deemed essential to combat the emerging recession. Foreign Minister Tonomura pursued regional integration initiatives that Economy Minister Akiyama viewed as disadvantageous to domestic manufacturers.

===The economic collapse===
Within weeks of Fukuda's appointment, the speculative bubble that had driven Okaiken's "Go-go 8090" prosperity definitively burst. The OSE 225 index, which had peaked at 39,000 on the final trading day of 1990, began a precipitous decline. By mid-April, the index had fallen below 30,000—a staggering 23% collapse that erased billions of yen in paper wealth.

Real estate values followed. Luxury condominiums in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]] that had sold for ¥100 million in 1989 found no buyers at ¥40 million by May 1991. Banks, their balance sheets ravaged by non-performing loans, sharply curtailed lending. The construction zaibatsu that had fueled the bubble with speculative projects began filing for bankruptcy protection.

Fukuda's government proved utterly incapable of responding. Emergency cabinet meetings devolved into shouting matches between Kamei's austerity faction and Takahata's stimulus proponents. A proposed ¥2 trillion emergency public works package died in committee when the Socialist Party threatened to withdraw from the coalition if the spending measure proceeded without corresponding tax increases.

===Coalition collapse===
The final collapse began on May 8, 1991, when Deputy Prime Minister and Finance Minister Ryōsuke Kamei, citing "irreconcilable differences regarding fiscal policy," formally announced the Socialist Party's withdrawal from the governing coalition. Without socialist support, Fukuda's government commanded only 187 of the 489 seats in the House of Representatives—far short of the majority required to pass legislation.

Efforts to persuade the Social Democrats or the New Trade Democrats to bridge the gap failed. By May 12, Fukuda had lost the confidence of the Diet, with opposition parties and former coalition partners alike calling for his resignation.

On May 15, 1991, after just 48 days in office—approximately seven weeks—Masaaki Fukuda submitted his resignation to Emperor Wakayama. He was succeeded by [[Michio Watanabe]], whose tenure would prove even shorter, lasting merely twelve days before giving way to [[Honda Shigehara]]'s return for a second term.

==Later life==
===Opposition leader===
Following his resignation, Fukuda remained as leader of Komeitō and assumed the role of Leader of the Opposition, a position he held until November 1993. His parliamentary performances against the Honda and Katō governments earned grudging respect even from opponents; his detailed interrogations of government witnesses during the 1992 Sumitomo Bank scandal hearings were widely praised for their forensic rigor.

However, the 1993 general election proved disastrous for Komeitō. The party lost 23 seats as voters abandoned centrist parties for either the resurgent Progressive Party or the rising Communist movement led by [[Sho Aoyagi]]. Fukuda resigned as party leader, accepting personal responsibility for the electoral collapse.

===Party merger and retirement===
In 1994, Fukuda engineered Komeitō's merger into the broader Progressive Party of Okaiken, serving briefly as the merged party's vice president under [[Naoto Emi]]. He declined to seek re-election in his Kagami 1st constituency in the 1995 general election, retiring from electoral politics after fifteen years in the Diet.

From his retirement, Fukuda occasionally contributed op-eds to major newspapers, primarily commenting on fiscal policy and administrative reform. He served on the board of several educational foundations and remained an influential behind-the-scenes figure in Kagami prefectural politics.

==Personal life==
Fukuda married {{nihongo|Yumiko Kagetora|武豊由美子|Kagetora Yumiko}} in 1968. She was the daughter of a retired naval officer from [[Mito Prefecture|Mito]]. The couple had three children:

* {{nihongo|Taro Fukuda|福田太郎|Fukuda Tarō}} (born 1970) – former member of the House of Representatives from Kagami 2nd district (2000–2012), prominent Progressive Party figure
* {{nihongo|Keiko Matsuzawa|松澤恵子|Matsuzawa Keiko}} (born 1973) – married to former cabinet secretary [[Hiroshi Matsuzawa]]'s son
* {{nihongo|Masato Fukuda|福田雅人|Fukuda Masato =}} (born 1977) – corporate lawyer, [[Aizawa]] office partner at Kagami & Associates
===Relationship with Takeo Fukuda==
Scholars have extensively analyzed Masaaki Fukuda's complex relationship with his father, Takeo Fukuda. While both men shared a commitment to administrative reform and clean government, their political careers diverged significantly. Takeo's premiership represented a high-water mark for the "Fukuda School" of fiscal conservatism, while Masaaki's brief tenure came to symbolize governmental paralysis.

Political biographer [[Kenji Watanabe]] noted in his 2001 study ''Sons of the Revolution: The Fukuda Dynasty'' that "Masaaki spent his entire career in his father's shadow—first seeking to escape it through independent achievement, then resigning himself to inheriting it as a burden rather than a blessing."

==Legacy==
Masaaki Fukuda's premiership, despite its brevity, marked a pivotal threshold in Okaiken's post-war history. His government's failure to address the opening stages of the economic collapse established a pattern of political paralysis that would characterize the entire [[Lost Decade|Lost Decades]] period from 1991 through 2005.

Historian [[Yoshimi Tanigawa]] of [[Toyozawa University]] assessed Fukuda's place in history bluntly:

{{quote|Fukuda didn't create the Lost Decades—he inherited their precondition. But his government's total inability to form consensus on even the most basic economic stabilization measures established the template for governmental dysfunction that would plague Okaiken for the next fifteen years. In this sense, his forty-eight days in office mattered far more than their brevity would suggest.}}

The "Fukuda Threshold"—the term political scientists use to describe the point at which a government loses functional capacity due to coalition fragmentation—was named in reference to his premiership. The concept entered standard Sanese political science curricula as a case study in coalition management failure.

In Kagami Prefecture, Fukuda remains a respected figure. The Fukuda Memorial Library in central Kagami, dedicated in 2005, houses archival materials from both Takeo and Masaaki Fukuda's political careers. His son [[Taro Fukuda]]'s election to the Diet in 2000 was widely attributed to lingering goodwill toward the family name among Kagami voters.

